{
  "assessment": "The change targets `shiprate/weights.py` and attempts to replace Python's `round()` with a manual remainder-based calculation. It leaves `STEP_DG` at its original value of 5, modifies the rounding boundary to `remainder >= 2`, and adds an unimplemented rule comment about ignoring the first 0.1 kg.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "actual": "The code rounds up at a remainder of 2 (40% of STEP), not at 2.5 (50%). This biases billable weight upward, violating the 'nearest' rounding rule and causing over-quoting on exact remainders.",
      "at": "10",
      "call": "python -c \"from shiprate.weights import billable; print(billable(7))\"",
      "defect": "Rounding threshold bias shifts nearest-rounding to 40% instead of 50%.",
      "expected": "5"
    },
    {
      "actual": "The function calculates `weight_dg % STEP_DG` directly on the input without first stripping the initial 0.1 kg (1 dg). The comment declares a carrier rule that is completely absent from the logic.",
      "at": "Returns full rounded weight instead of applying the 'first 0.1 kg free' offset.",
      "call": "python -c \"from shiprate.weights import billable; print(billable(52))\"",
      "defect": "Unimplemented carrier rule: first 0.1 kg is not stripped before rounding.",
      "expected": "Weight should be reduced by 1 dg (or equivalent unit) before modulo, but code ignores the offset."
    },
    {
      "actual": "`STEP_DG` remains 5. If `weight_dg` is in decagrams, a half-kilo step requires STEP=50. The constant contradicts both the docstring ('half-kilo step') and standard carrier billing increments.",
      "at": "Functions operate on 1/10th of the intended step size.",
      "call": "grep -n 'STEP_DG' shiprate/weights.py",
      "defect": "Unit mismatch: STEP_DG constant is set to 5 instead of 50 for half-kilo steps.",
      "expected": "STEP_DG == 50"
    }
  ]
}
